  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 246 AM CDT Sat Oct 17 2026\n\nSurface high pressure is dominating the region with clear skies and \nnearly calm winds allowing temperatures to drop into the 30s early \nthis morning. With a few observations already very near freezing, we \nare on track to see lows in the low to mid 30s, coldest within the \nFreeze Warning area. As the sfc high moves east today, a return to \nsouthwest winds under abundant sunshine will help highs reach a few \ndegrees on either side of 60 this afternoon. Eastern portions of the \narea may still manage to fall to the mid 30s by Sunday morning, so \ncan't rule out some more patchy frost in those locations, especially \nin lower lying valleys. A more notable warming trend begins Sunday \nas a Pacific upper low weakens and phases with the southwest flow \naloft, inducing a lee surface trough in response and strengthening \nthe pressure gradient. As a result, breezier southwest winds should \nboost temperatures into the mid to upper 60s for most of the area. \nThe shortwave energy from the weakening system aloft passes through \nthe region early next week, though it may stay mainly north of us \nand limited moisture may also work against rainfall, so a dry \nforecast holds for now.\n\nThe upper pattern becomes more amplified by the middle of the week \nwith a deepening trough off the Pacific coast and a strengthening \nridge over the central CONUS. This favors continued warmth with \ntemperatures returning to above average. Highs climb each day, \nthrough the 70s at the start of the work week and eventually 80s by \nThursday (possibly Wednesday for some). A small temperature range in \nthe ensemble guidance plus good agreement among ensemble clusters is \nindicative of high confidence in the medium range. Some subtle \ndifferences in the strength and timing of the western trough are \nnoted towards the end of the forecast period, and as a result rain \nchances remain low (20% or less) Friday before increasing into next \nweekend.",
